<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  2026-10-19 - com.apple.developer.associated-domains removed (HH-160).

    The 2026-09-22 bundle ID migration added an Associated Domain
    (applinks:simplewithus.com, webcredentials:simplewithus.com).  It made
    the app unlaunchable, because that entitlement is restricted: macOS only
    honours it when a Developer ID provisioning profile carrying it is
    embedded at Contents/embedded.provisionprofile.  This project signs with
    CODE_SIGN_STYLE Automatic and embeds no profile, so every launch died
    before main:

      taskgated-helper: Unsatisfied entitlements: com.apple.developer.associated-domains
      amfid:            AppleMobileFileIntegrityError -413 "No matching profile found"
      launchd:          NSPOSIXErrorDomain 163 "Launchd job spawn failed"

    A strict deep codesign verify still passed, and Gatekeeper still accepted
    the bundle, so the fault was invisible until the process was actually
    spawned.  Finder's only clue was the generic "The application cannot be
    opened" dialog, which sends you looking at the bundle instead of the
    launch log.  Do not add this entitlement back without the profile.

    Nothing used it.  There is no NSUserActivity, applinks, webcredentials,
    or associatedDomain anywhere in Sources/ or ios/Sources/, and the
    apple-app-site-association file it depended on was never hosted, so the
    domain could not have validated even if the process had started.

    To restore it, do all three or the app goes dead again:

      1. Enable the Associated Domains capability on the App ID
         com.simplewithus.hoghunter.macos in the Apple Developer Portal.
      2. Add a Developer ID Application provisioning profile that carries the
         entitlement, embed it in the bundle, and reference it from
         project.yml so that every build ships it.  The profile is the part
         that is easy to forget: the portal capability alone does nothing.
      3. Host https://simplewithus.com/.well-known/apple-app-site-association
         naming the bundle ID, or applinks stays a no-op.

  com.apple.security.application-groups    -> [group.com.simplewithus.hoghunter]
    New App Group from the 2026-09-22 migration, and this one IS load
    bearing: DiskCleaner, CleanRegimen and InfisicalSettings all read
    UserDefaults(suiteName:) from it, InfisicalSettings resolves its shared
    container URL from it, and the iPhone companion writes the same suite.
    Removing it would silently break Mac-to-iPhone state sharing, so it
    stays.  taskgated-helper accepts it without a provisioning profile, which
    is the one asymmetry that made the other entitlement so hard to spot.

    Existing local state lives under ~/Library/Logs/HogHunter/ and the
    internal Application Support directory "HogHunter"; renaming those would
    orphan user history, so they are intentionally NOT renamed.

    NOTE: this is an XML comment, so it may never contain a double hyphen.
    Writing a command flag like codesign -verify -deep inside one produces
    "Failed to parse entitlements: AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error" at
    signing time, and the build silently falls back to an unusable adhoc
    signature.  Spell flags without the leading dashes.

  See docs/rollouts/2026-09-22-bundle-id-migration.md for the full
  fleet-wide migration context.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.com.simplewithus.hoghunter</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>